        <title>Writing As An Engineer (in the AI Era)</title>
        <published>2026-06-13T00:00:00+00:00</published>
        <updated>2026-06-13T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
        
        <author>
          <name>
            
              Brandon Hurst
            
          </name>
        </author>
        
        <link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://brandon-hurst.github.io/blog/1-writing-as-an-engineer/"/>
        <id>https://brandon-hurst.github.io/blog/1-writing-as-an-engineer/</id>
        
        <content type="html" xml:base="https://brandon-hurst.github.io/blog/1-writing-as-an-engineer/">&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Okay, so this is my first blog post ever. You&#x27;ve been warned -- I&#x27;m new at this!&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;who-am-i&quot;&gt;Who Am I?&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;&#x2F;blog&#x2F;yellowstone-selfie-aug2023.png&quot; alt=&quot;Me at Yellowstone&quot; width=300 style=&quot;display:block; margin:auto;&quot; &#x2F;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am an embedded systems engineer with a background in electronics. I also love to play guitar (and, albeit sparingly, piano). I love music and movies with a burning passion, and I very much consider myself a technical creative. This will primarily be a technical blog, but I reserve the right to include musings on just about anything. Read on for a manifesto of sorts...&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;why-start-a-blog&quot;&gt;Why Start a Blog?&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I&#x27;m making a blog. Why am I doing this? There are many reasons it is an overtly foolish idea these days. To name a few...&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Writing on the internet is easily generated like never before, technical and otherwise&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lots of tech people have a lot of reading to do. What makes what I have to say worth hearing?&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All of my words will just get fed into the latest AI, my expertise anonymized, and my name will disappear from my knowledge forever&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The fact of the matter is I really do love to write. I always said if I wasn&#x27;t an engineer, I would&#x27;ve been an English teacher. Like many, I feel I could&#x27;ve walked down a lot of paths in life, but writing is one thing I still consider a sacred act. Be it my lab notes, a journal, a text, or even (yes) an email, I believe that writing is one of the most fulfilling things we can spend our time doing. I don&#x27;t even feel like I should bother explaining it here...in some sense, you get it or you don&#x27;t. It helps me work out how I&#x27;m feeling, what I&#x27;ve learned, what I&#x27;ve yet to learn, and the artifact of it is a snapshot of my mind.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Think with me for a second...we humans are the most mentally sophisticated creatures that our immediate universe is known to hold. Each one of us is capable of leaving behind -countless- snapshots of our thinking and context at any given time. From a purely selfish perspective, I&#x27;d say that I &lt;em&gt;can&#x27;t wait&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; to get older and look back to see moments like this, when I decided a thought was worth capturing.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;SO, that&#x27;s why I&#x27;m writing. Despite the fact that a robot will take it all away from me. Despite the fact that the tech world is increasingly losing its passion for humans who can hold a system in their heads, or who can celebrate humanity and work for its growth. I want to be one of those people. And my hope is that, whether this is read directly by a human or delivered by a robot, maybe it helps someone grow along with me.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I will say, I don&#x27;t expect a lot of readership -- and that&#x27;s fine! This is primarily so I have a place to collect and record things I work on and think are valuable. So I can share it with my family and friends, and one day, myself. If you find it interesting, though, please feel free to reach out :)&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;AI be damned, I&#x27;m still gonna write!&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;what-s-it-going-to-look-like&quot;&gt;What&#x27;s It Going to Look Like?&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The content will mostly be about embedded systems. Design of boards, programming of microcontrollers (with predominantly C &#x2F; C++ and hopefully some Rust), book reviews, embedded Linux, etc. But I&#x27;m reserving the right to write about music as well if the mood strikes! We&#x27;ll see. At this point, who can say for sure anyhow?&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;special-shout-outs&quot;&gt;Special Shout-Outs&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I will list in brief here some of the folks that influenced me to do this.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chris Gammell of the Amp Hour&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Elecia White of Embedded.fm &#x2F; Logical Elegance&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bryan Cantrill &amp;amp; Adam Leventhal of Oxide Computer&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Nathan Jones, Security Educator, Writer at Digikey, Consultant&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
</content>
        
